Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["\<\
RIES matches for the fine-structure constant within its error bar\
\>", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"cd67284c-e433-db4a-8c16-a9065f27a0c2"],

Cell["\<\
Target T = 0.0072973525643, CODATA 2022 uncertainty sigma = 1.1e-12 (relative \
1.5e-10). RIES 2026-05-06, level 5, default symbols. 66 equations.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ab509436-d467-464a-bbb1-195af4aa5b12"],

Cell["\<\
ries -F3 -x -l5 --no-refinement --no-slow-messages -n1000000 \
--max-match-distance -1.5e-10 0.0072973525643\
\>", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"16ee3fc0-29a6-8b4e-9f38-4e6ec275a930"],

Cell["\<\
How RIES finds these: it builds left-hand sides that contain x (evaluated at \
x = T, together with their derivative d/dx) and right-hand sides that are \
constants only, and matches the two sets; a close pair is solved for x with \
Newton's method. --max-match-distance -1.5e-10 means: report an equation only \
if its root x lies within 1.5e-10 * T of the target T (a negative value is \
relative). It is the distance between the root and the target, not between \
the values of the two sides. --no-refinement reports every such equation \
(without it, each new match must be closer than the previous one). The \
complexity score is RIES's own weighting of the symbols used; many equations \
are equivalent to each other.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"23adeea2-70e3-8342-bdf0-85420b4ef59a"],

Cell["\<\
Equations solved for x with FindRoot at 40 digits; (x - T)/sigma is the \
distance from the target in units of the CODATA uncertainty (|value| <= 1.36 \
by construction).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1b2f4628-c57f-4f42-8fac-de20b7a451cc"],

